      "content": "it's just hard to believe it's just coincidence when the timing is so surgical. even if there isn't a paper with that exact name, i've read about how certain frequencies can be used in crowd control or \"non-lethal\" applications to influence mood. i'm an architect so i know how these smart buildings are integrated—everything is connected to a central nervous system of sensors and automation. it wouldn't be that hard to program a subtle pattern into the HVAC cycle. \n\nif i wanted to test this myself, without letting management know i'm onto them, what kind of improvised shielding could i use in my apartment to block out these frequencies? like, i need to see if the physical sensation actually drops when i'm behind some kind of acoustic barrier. do you know if certain materials or even specific layout changes could dampen infrasound?",
